{#-
  Chat template for yanolja/YanoljaNEXT-Rosetta-*, used only when
  PROMPT_FORMAT=rosetta.

  Kept here as a readable reference. The Dockerfile does NOT copy this file -
  it writes an identical copy to /app/chat-template-rosetta.jinja inline via
  a RUN heredoc, so the image build never depends on this file being present
  in whatever context produced it (a plain `git push` always has it; a manual
  file-by-file upload to a Space is what missed it once already). If you
  change the template, update both this file and the Dockerfile's copy.

  The template embedded in that GGUF cannot be used: it calls the Jinja
  `default` filter on an object, which llama.cpp's minja engine does not
  implement, so `llama-server --jinja` aborts at startup with
  "Unknown (built-in) filter 'default' for type Object" and the server never
  comes up. This is a minja-compatible rewrite producing byte-identical
  output for the one-system-plus-one-user requests this API sends: it drops
  the selectattr/default chains for plain loops.

  Role mapping matches the original: system -> instruction, user -> source,
  and the generation prompt opens a translation turn.
-#}
{{- bos_token -}}
{%- for m in messages -%}
{%- if m['role'] == 'system' -%}
{{- '<start_of_turn>instruction\n' -}}{{- m['content'] | trim -}}{{- '<end_of_turn>\n' -}}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endfor -%}
{%- for m in messages -%}
{%- if m['role'] == 'user' -%}
{{- '<start_of_turn>source\n' -}}{{- m['content'] | trim -}}{{- '<end_of_turn>\n' -}}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endfor -%}
{%- if add_generation_prompt -%}
{{- '<start_of_turn>translation\n' -}}
{%- endif -%}
